      "worked": "Since $x^2\\ge0$ for every real $x$,\n$$\\begin{align*}1+x^2&\\ge1.\\end{align*}$$\nThe denominator is never zero, so $D=(-\\infty,\\infty)$.\n\nThe denominator takes every value in $[1,\\infty)$, so its reciprocal takes every value in $(0,1]$:\n$$\\begin{align*}0<\\frac{1}{1+x^2}&\\le1.\\end{align*}$$\nThe upper endpoint is attained at $x=0$, where $f(0)=1$; zero is never attained. Therefore $R=(0,1]$.",
